    desc: "Formally catalogued under the binomial <em>Vetercetus primordium</em>. Internally, personnel refer to the organism informally as 'The King of the Seas,' or, more commonly in day-to-day correspondence, simply 'The Leviathan' — neither term appears in any official classification document, but both are used consistently enough across departments that I.R.I.S. communications style guides now permit their use in non-formal internal reporting. A marine organism of a scale that renders conventional biological measurement impractical; no complete specimen, living or deceased, has ever been recovered or fully surveyed. Pronounced sexual dimorphism has been established from partial remains and comparative measurement: females are confirmed to be the larger sex by a substantial margin, while males carry a pair of cranial horn structures believed to function defensively, absent in females. Both sexes are confirmed capable of asexual reproduction, though the two follow markedly different behavioral profiles — females are documented as comparatively more passive and, per internal fertility assessment, notably more fertile; males are more territorial and aggressive, a temperament correlated with measurably lower fertility across the specimens assessed. I.R.I.S. has no confirmed explanation for the fertility correlation and treats it as observational only. Between 1988 and 2005, I.R.I.S. conducted five separate physical analysis attempts on partial remains believed to belong to TVP-005. Only two of the five expeditions returned. Surviving records indicate all five were staged from vessels operating at or near the primary Pacific Null Zone (see: TNZ-559), targeting fragments recovered near the seafloor perimeter rather than any attempt at direct approach to a living specimen. No distress signal, wreckage, or debris field was ever recovered from any of the three lost expeditions; all three are formally listed as lost with no further detail available at this clearance level. Results from the two recovered samples were consistent with each other: certain integumentary layers tested as measurably older than the confirmed geological age of Earth's crust, and exhibited a cellular density inconsistent with any known biological tissue, living or fossilized. I.R.I.S. has no working theory that accounts for either finding and treats both as unverified pending further study. The organism's proper habitat and range are unknown; oceanic drag markings consistent with a single massive entity have been documented in proximity to multiple Null Zone sites (see: TNZ-559), suggesting periodic movement between locations rather than fixed residence at any one. No confirmed direct sighting of a living adult specimen exists in I.R.I.S. records. All physical evidence to date is either partial remains, drag markings, or the fossil-embedded fragment described below. Internal threat assessment protocol for a hypothetical direct encounter has never progressed past a theoretical drafting stage, as no containment or engagement strategy proposed to date has been considered credible against an organism of this scale. Confirmed skeletal remains — two ribs, each split laterally in half — have been identified embedded within the interior of several peaks of Mount Everest, indistinguishable at a glance from the region's well-documented marine fossil deposits (a known consequence of the Tethys Ocean uplift during the Himalayan orogeny). Comparative analysis attributes the fragments to a single female specimen, currently the oldest confirmed individual on record. The fragment's true nature was identified only after cross-referencing anomalous density readings against unrelated mountaineering survey data; no dedicated I.R.I.S. expedition to the site has been mounted, both for jurisdictional reasons and due to the site's extreme inaccessibility relative to its documentation priority. Severely deteriorated Sirena remains were recovered in close proximity to the fragments; the circumstances of their deaths are unrecorded, and no theory as to how marine organisms reached that elevation, living or dead, has been substantiated. TVP-005 is believed responsible for a single confirmed historical awakening event, internally designated the Maritime Pangaea — a large-scale, sudden oceanic disturbance that is understood to have physically divided a then-inhabited landmass, sending one fragment adrift and driving the other into the Antarctic, where it remains partially submerged and frozen (cross-reference: Region-3 pre-Halley surface anomalies, clearance Level 4 required). The organism's current status following this event has never been confirmed; whether it remains dormant, active, or deceased is unknown. The continued presence of fresh drag markings near active Null Zone sites, some dated within the last several decades, is treated internally as the strongest available evidence against a deceased status, though it is not considered conclusive. Cross-reference, TBL-007: the persistently low confirmed population of TBL-007 relative to its position in the local food web is treated internally as indirect evidence that TVP-005 preys on the species, despite the total absence of recovered physical remains supporting this theory. A secondary, unconfirmed hypothesis under internal review holds that predation on TBL-007 may not be purely nutritional: TBL-007 individuals are confirmed to periodically discharge a toxic, acidic secretion known as the Black Tide, which has been logged in proximity to multiple Null Zone sites and poses a measurable contamination risk to TVP-005 nesting territory and the surrounding ecosystem more broadly. Whether TVP-005 actively targets TBL-007 as a means of ecological self-preservation, does so incidentally while feeding, or the correlation is coincidental, has not been established, and I.R.I.S. considers all three explanations equally unproven at this time. Addendum, unconfirmed: a minority position within the analysis team has floated the theory that TVP-005 may not be a singular organism at all in the sense the term usually implies, but rather the oldest known survivor of a once-larger population — a theory drawn entirely from the sheer improbability of a species surviving to the present day as a lone individual, and one the broader research division has declined to endorse pending literally any supporting evidence whatsoever."
