    desc: "A designation applied to a series of anomalous deep-ocean depressions identified across multiple oceanic regions, characterized by a complete and inexplicable absence of biological activity, geological movement, and any measurable organic compound below a certain depth threshold. The term 'Null Zone' was coined by I.R.I.S. field teams during initial survey operations; no official scientific designation exists outside of I.R.I.S. documentation. All confirmed Null Zones share a consistent structural profile: a large circular depression in the ocean floor, sloping inward toward a central point of absolute void. The geometry is consistent with ancient meteorite impact craters from Earth's early formation period. <span class=\"redact-block\">This hypothesis is no longer considered the leading explanation. Cross-departmental review, prompted by confirmed cross-reference material on TVP-005 and TPI-003, now treats Null Zones as probable nesting sites — the surrounding absence of ordinary biological activity attributed not to a sterilizing impact residue, but to the surrounding ecosystem having been systematically depleted or actively avoided over an extended period, consistent with apex predation on a scale local marine life cannot sustain. The original impact-crater hypothesis has not been formally withdrawn, as the two explanations are not mutually exclusive.</span> The leading internal sub-hypothesis holds that the impacting bodies deposited a substance or property — not yet isolated, identified, or replicated under laboratory conditions — that permanently prevents the formation or sustenance of life within the affected radius. I.R.I.S. has not confirmed this. The primary Null Zone, located in the Pacific Ocean, is by a substantial margin the largest confirmed instance. Its full depth is approximately 11,230 meters, consistent with a crater of extraordinary scale. I.R.I.S. conducted three years of remote observation and unmanned submersible survey before committing personnel to the site. During this period, a surface platform designated El Oasis was constructed directly above the Zone to serve as the primary operational hub. El Oasis currently houses 24 personnel and serves as the logistical and communications anchor for all sub-surface research operations. At approximately 8,975 meters depth — roughly 5,000 meters below the operational entry threshold — unmanned submersibles confirmed the presence of a large partially-embedded structure designated internally as La Esponja de Hielo. <span class=\"redact-block\">La Esponja de Hielo has since been reclassified: it is not a mineral or impact-ejecta formation, but a colony of several individually massive coral organisms, living. Interior cavities have been confirmed to house both TVP-005 egg clusters and resident TPI-003 colonies actively guarding them. The structure's sustained 60°C surface emission, previously unexplained, is now understood as a biological incubation function rather than a passive thermal anomaly.</span> Water temperature in the immediate vicinity of the structure registers at 34–35°C, a localized thermal anomaly that does not propagate beyond a contained radius. It has not been approached by crewed vehicles. <span class=\"redact-block\">Oceanic drag markings consistent with a single entity of extreme mass have been documented at multiple Null Zone sites in recent survey passes, suggesting periodic visitation by an adult TVP-005 rather than fixed residence at any single location.</span> Additional Null Zones of significantly smaller scale have been confirmed in other regions, including formations near the Japanese archipelago (Region-6), in the North Pacific above the Russian eastern coastline (Region-11), and a smaller instance in the South Atlantic designated the Malvinas Null Zone, located at the operational boundary between Region-3 and Region-13. The Malvinas formation is of secondary historical significance: its associated surface platform served as the primary evacuation point for surviving personnel following the 2003 Halley IV incident. Internal note: three years of unmanned survey of the primary Pacific Null Zone produced no biological readings, no geological activity, and no anomalous energy signatures of any kind. The submersibles returned with footage of water. Forty-seven hours of footage of water. This is what convinced the committee to send people down."
